#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078149 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20b289d4-96a7-5d1b-9714-6afabab4804d" :knowledge-point/title "Further Determining Rates of Change in Geometrical Settings"}] :question/id #uuid "a3234c09-08eb-56f2-8a4c-d6430d38c356" :question/math-academy-id "e-275" :question/problem "A spherical balloon is being inflated so that its volume is increasing. Find the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the balloon is changing with respect to the radius at the moment when the diameter of the balloon is equal to $12\\, \\text{cm}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/620/Source/Images/038c730db46c27977ecaf5b36ce200bc.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that the diameter of the balloon is $12\\, \\text{cm}$, its radius is\n\n$$\nr =6\\, \\text{cm}\n$$\n\nNow, we know that the volume of a sphere with radius $r$ is given by\n\n$$\nV= V(r) = \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $V(r)$ with respect to $r$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} V'(r) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d r}\\left(\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3\\right) \\\\[5pt] & = \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi (3r^2) \\\\[5pt] &= 4\\pi r^2, \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $r=6$ gives\n\n$$\nV'(6)= 4\\pi (6)^2= 144\\pi\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the balloon is changing when its diameter is $12\\, \\text{cm}\\,$ is $\\, 144\\pi \\, \\text{cm}^3/\\text{cm}$.\n\n**Note:** We read $\\text{cm}^3/\\text{cm}$ as \"centimeters cubed per centimeter\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078150 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff5ec0b-1456-5231-94e1-0fc5923d6672" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "3c4f9912-4b08-5e11-8ef6-4e4ee35765c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-276" :question/problem "Lizzie buys $b$ pounds of bananas at a price $p$, in dollars, that can be modeled by the function $p(b)=5e^{-0.2b}$. What is the instantaneous rate of change of the price when $b=5$ pounds?" :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change of the price when $b=5$ pounds is equal to $p'(5)$.\n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np^{′}(b) &= \\frac{d}{db}(5e^{-0.2b}) \\\\\n&= 5e^{-0.2b} \\cdot (-0.2) \\\\\n&=-e^{-0.2b}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $b=5$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np^{′}(5) &= -e^{-0.2(5)} \\\\\n&=-e^{-1} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{e}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate change of the price when $b=5$ pounds is\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{e}\n$$\n\ndollars per pound."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078251 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "236cec41-4836-5526-893d-e6f2a23d1801" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function"}] :question/id #uuid "f2eda994-e00a-5e9b-a26d-720d01e6fa07" :question/math-academy-id "e-6174" :question/problem "A particle moves in a straight line. After $t$ seconds the particle is $\\, f(t)=2t^3 - 5t + 9\\,$ feet away from its initial position. What is the instantaneous rate of change of the particle's position as a function of $t$?" :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change of the particle's position is equal to $f'(t)$.\n\nWe take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(2t^{3} - 5t + 9) \\\\\n&= 6t^{2} - 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $t$ seconds, the instantaneous rate of change of the particle's position is $6t^2 - 5$ feet per second."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259051 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20b289d4-96a7-5d1b-9714-6afabab4804d" :knowledge-point/title "Further Determining Rates of Change in Geometrical Settings"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77cfab07-1ced-53a5-9c30-1fdc71ce1fe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271557} {:answer/id #uuid "596201c2-5166-51c3-b347-e55714af0c78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271558} {:answer/id #uuid "858df574-af1c-5332-ad4c-76bba9f41ed4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271559} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb6371d-dc04-577b-ac3c-59dd08d86b48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271560} {:answer/id #uuid "7d6a33ce-6a60-5b2d-9310-cd5ffd8b2945" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "596201c2-5166-51c3-b347-e55714af0c78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271558} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb77417a-e174-52a2-acff-95872f70db10" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274589}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93b93b20-63af-5abb-b512-c02cecaedf81" :question/math-academy-id "q-2545" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/f59f65e184ccf4fdf2ae2124543ece45d41afce09d743dec9f17730dd00124d4.png)\n\nThe area $A$ of the circle above is increasing. The circumference and radius of the circle are $C$ and $r$ respectively. \nFind the rate of change of the area of the circle with respect to its circumference." :question/worked-solution "The area of a circle is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the circle.\n\nWe know that $C=2\\pi r.$ so we can express the area $A$ as a function of the circumference $C$ by substituting $r=\\frac{C}{2\\pi }$ in the formula above. We get\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $A(C)$ with respect to $C$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{'}(C) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}({C}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi }(2C) \\\\  & =\\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286679 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20b289d4-96a7-5d1b-9714-6afabab4804d" :knowledge-point/title "Further Determining Rates of Change in Geometrical Settings"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7874d3c1-1e7d-5935-918f-98a39cbf1ad9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2s^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286681} {:answer/id #uuid "15d53ce7-5bf9-5cbc-801d-aca9e585a89b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6s^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7874d3c1-1e7d-5935-918f-98a39cbf1ad9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2s^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286681} :answer-field/id #uuid "a76f6768-6175-5831-9bef-cf3c3433a53d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef0e7ebd-8eb3-5e6b-8197-77c0558d373a" :question/math-academy-id "q-313364" :question/problem "The volume of a right square pyramid is increasing. Given that its height $h$ is always twice the length of a base, find the rate of change of the volume of the pyramid with respect to its base length $s.$\n\n*Hint*: The volume of a right square pyramid is $V=\\frac{1}{3}{s}^{2}h.$ \n\nThe rate of change of the pyramid’s volume with respect to $s$ is \n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We are given that $h=2s,$ so we can express the volume of the pyramid $V$ as a function of the base edge length $s$ by substituting $h=2s$ in the formula of the volume. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(s) & =\\frac{1}{3}{s}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{s}^{2}(2s) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{s}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $V(s)$ with respect to $s$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{d}{ds}\\,(\\frac{2}{3}{s}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{d}{ds}\\,({s}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 3{s}^{2} \\\\  & =2{s}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the rate of change of the pyramid’s volume with respect to its base length $s$ is $2{s}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266159 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff5ec0b-1456-5231-94e1-0fc5923d6672" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55742b20-d2eb-52dd-93ba-2bea32a81289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55742b20-d2eb-52dd-93ba-2bea32a81289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266161} :answer-field/id #uuid "181d8716-3c0a-5aff-9c35-c6ef0ef17dc0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bcb5129b-3804-548c-a47a-d6b56b122986" :question/math-academy-id "q-343424" :question/problem "The concentration $C$ (in millimoles per liter) of a chemical in a reactor $t$ minutes after the reaction begins is modeled by the function $C(t)={t}^{3}+25$ for $0\\le t\\le 10.$ Find the instantaneous rate at which the concentration is changing with respect to time at the moment when the concentration is $150$ millimoles per liter.\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change when the concentration is $150$ millimoles per liter is\n{{field-1}} millimoles per liter per minute." :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that the chemical's concentration is $150$ millimoles per liter, the time can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(t) & =150 \\\\ {t}^{3}+25 & =150 \\\\ {t}^{3} & =125 \\\\ t & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe instantaneous rate at which the chemical's concentration is changing when $t=5$ minutes is equal to ${C}^{′}(5).$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $C(t)$ with respect to $t$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+25) \\\\  & =3{t}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $t=5$ gives\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(5)=3(5)^{2}=75.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the chemical's concentration when its concentration is $150$ millimoles per liter is  $75$ millimoles per liter per minute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266166 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20b289d4-96a7-5d1b-9714-6afabab4804d" :knowledge-point/title "Further Determining Rates of Change in Geometrical Settings"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbe2982f-a752-54b7-b0f4-c50b37c260b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbe2982f-a752-54b7-b0f4-c50b37c260b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266168} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e113cfa-03c5-531e-9d48-c21ef10c1193" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ce974f27-56cb-5d6d-a571-c1f4112d1075" :question/math-academy-id "q-343502" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953428/assets/5fc1aff711355a147abac33a513434c55575c552ab58f0164ebc87222530f371.png)\n\nThe volume of a cube-shaped container is increasing. Find the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the container is changing with respect to the side length at the moment when the face diagonal of the cube is equal to $10\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change at the moment when the face diagonal is $10\\sqrt{2}$ centimeters is equal to\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{cm}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that the face diagonal of the cube is $10\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cm}$, its side length $s$ is $10\\,\\text{cm}.$ Now, the volume of a cube with side length $s$ is given by \n\n$$\nV=V(s)={s}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $V(s)$ with respect to $s$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}({s}^{3}) \\\\  & =3{s}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $s=10$ gives\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(10)=3(10)^{2}=300.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the container is increasing when the face diagonal is $10\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cm}$ is $300\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{cm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260569 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff5ec0b-1456-5231-94e1-0fc5923d6672" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f825c9f6-fa66-5744-9891-f93ad569990e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(1\\,699)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272350} {:answer/id #uuid "3e4ae87d-ca20-5ec3-ab30-b42ad74b467a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(1\\,699)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272351} {:answer/id #uuid "0a9884b4-5900-5d9b-a23f-dcb291c1bb95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(1\\,699)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272352} {:answer/id #uuid "42090516-9715-5392-8c32-b922ea5ea87d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,699" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272353} {:answer/id #uuid "680464eb-4cd1-5cb5-93cc-7c8e95b9261f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(1\\,699)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42090516-9715-5392-8c32-b922ea5ea87d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,699" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272353} :answer-field/id #uuid "82a88222-952e-5819-9181-f73b5a076050" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4df84652-8096-5b0a-ac62-74634ce27764" :question/math-academy-id "q-48700" :question/problem "The number of computers affected by the spread of some ransomware after $t$ days can be modeled by the function $f(t)=\\frac{100\\,({t}^{3}+2)}{t+1}.$ What is the instantaneous rate of change in the number of infected computers when $t=9$ days?" :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change in the spread when $t=9$ days is equal to ${f}^{'}(9).$ \n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{100\\,({t}^{3}+2)}{t+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{100(3{t}^{2})(t+1)-100({t}^{3}+2)(1)}{{(t+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =100\\cdot \\frac{2{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}-2}{{(t+1)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=9,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(9) & =100\\cdot \\frac{2(9)^{3}+3(9)^{2}-2}{{((9)+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =100\\cdot \\frac{1\\,458+243-2}{100} \\\\  & =1\\,699.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change in the spread when $t=9$ is $1\\,699$ computers per day."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262336 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff5ec0b-1456-5231-94e1-0fc5923d6672" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff3ea3a0-0252-57e3-aeec-232b99135da4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ meters per second" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262338} {:answer/id #uuid "96f05212-4e3a-599e-92ef-43d04918b0f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ meter per second" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262339} {:answer/id #uuid "1e3f02ef-59c7-5837-a6c2-9d92c02c90d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ meters per second" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262340} {:answer/id #uuid "b8fb16e8-cf8a-5273-9f9e-97b4fbe73eba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ meters per second" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262341} {:answer/id #uuid "0445507a-2423-5178-bf12-706b5c412c22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ meters per second" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff3ea3a0-0252-57e3-aeec-232b99135da4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ meters per second" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262338} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e1d0e37-098a-5943-85a3-515d49a429e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2e527e4e-1ea7-5d39-a401-6a8d7982a17f" :question/math-academy-id "q-72352" :question/problem "The function $x(t)={t}^{2}+t$ models the distance traveled by a car $t$ seconds after it sets off on a journey. Find the instantaneous rate at which the distance traveled by the car is changing with respect to time when $t=1$ second." :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate at which the distance traveled by the car is changing when $t=1$ second is equal to ${x}^{′}(1).$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $x(t)$ with respect to $t$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t)=2t+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $t=1$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(1)=2(1)+1=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the distance traveled by the car is changing when $t=1$ second is $3$ meters per second."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262350 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "236cec41-4836-5526-893d-e6f2a23d1801" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a52c4689-da7a-570e-8989-65b6edf76e48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{710}{1+{e}^{-t}}$  liters per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262352} {:answer/id #uuid "27d04d21-825b-59ef-bea0-e7136ff9cae3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{355}{1+{e}^{-t}}$ liters per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262353} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b91f08-9fb4-5b3f-9b4a-1814f2033caf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$355{e}^{t}$  liters per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262354} {:answer/id #uuid "db14086d-d818-5ca4-a619-9770d3a947e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{710{e}^{t}}{2+{e}^{-t}}$  liters per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262355} {:answer/id #uuid "43741ee7-1f7c-5b81-9f09-2b2714183729" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$710{e}^{-t}$  liters per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a52c4689-da7a-570e-8989-65b6edf76e48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{710}{1+{e}^{-t}}$  liters per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262352} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fde2b24-adb2-54a1-9c8d-6b66f11b4bbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1a7b751-463a-5c53-9c04-73eeed9f0b75" :question/math-academy-id "q-86191" :question/problem "The function $P(t)=710\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{e}^{t})$ represents the volume of milk, in liters, produced on a farm $t$ days after the owner bought new cows. What is the instantaneous rate of change of the volume of produced milk as a function of $t?$" :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change of the volume of produced milk with respect to time is ${P}^{′}(t).$\n\nNow, the rate of change of $P(t)$ with respect to $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(710\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{e}^{t})) \\\\  & =710\\,\\frac{{e}^{t}}{1+{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{710}{1+{e}^{-t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the volume of produced milk is  $\\frac{710}{1+{e}^{-t}}$ liters per day."}]}
